    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7e8df944-7799-50b9-88bb-23835fb89bce",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are fifth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{3}={x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{4}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fifth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
