      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of seventh-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
